<article class="env-lemma" id="0BU5">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0BU5">Lemma <span data-tag="0BU5">15.114.8</span>.</a> With assumptions and notation as in Lemma <a href="/tag/09EE" data-tag="09EE">15.114.5</a>. The inertia character $\theta : I \to \mu _ e(\kappa (\mathfrak m))$ satisfies the following property </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \theta (\tau \sigma \tau ^{-1}) = \tau (\theta (\sigma ))  \]
</div>
  <p> for $\tau \in D$ and $\sigma \in I$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The formula makes sense as $I$ is a normal subgroup of $D$ and as $\tau $ acts on $\kappa (\mathfrak m)$ via the map $D \to \text{Aut}(\kappa (\mathfrak m))$ discussed in Lemma <a href="/tag/09ED" data-tag="09ED">15.114.4</a> for example. Recall the construction of $\theta $. Choose a uniformizer $\pi $ of $B_\mathfrak m$ and for $\sigma \in I$ write $\sigma (\pi ) = \theta _\sigma \pi $. Then $\theta (\sigma )$ is the image $\overline{\theta }_\sigma $ of $\theta _\sigma $ in the residue field. For any $\tau \in D$ we can write $\tau (\pi ) = \theta _\tau \pi $ for some unit $\theta _\tau $. Then $\theta _{\tau ^{-1}} = \tau ^{-1}(\theta _\tau ^{-1})$. We compute 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  \theta _{\tau \sigma \tau ^{-1}} &  = \tau (\sigma (\tau ^{-1}(\pi )))/\pi \\ &  = \tau (\sigma (\tau ^{-1}(\theta _\tau ^{-1}) \pi ))/\pi \\ &  = \tau (\sigma (\tau ^{-1}(\theta _\tau ^{-1})) \theta _\sigma \pi )/\pi \\ &  = \tau (\sigma (\tau ^{-1}(\theta _\tau ^{-1}))) \tau (\theta _\sigma ) \theta _\tau \end{align*}
</div>
    <p>
       However, since $\sigma $ acts trivially modulo $\pi $ we see that the product $\tau (\sigma (\tau ^{-1}(\theta _\tau ^{-1}))) \theta _\tau $ maps to $1$ in the residue field. This proves the lemma. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>